          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think It Over"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.55,
            0.257,
            0.691
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why does a canoe move forward when the canoeist pushes water backwards with their paddle and why does it move faster when they push harder?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.08,
            0.693,
            0.264,
            0.848
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y Suppose the same canoeist uses the same paddle force in two different canoes, one empty and one carrying another passenger. In which case will the canoe move faster?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.303,
            0.517,
            0.891,
            0.639
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Chapter 4, you learnt to describe the motion of an object in terms of its position, velocity and acceleration. But you did not consider what causes motion. Is there an underlying cause for a change in position and velocity of an object? What is the nature of this cause? Do all motions require a cause? In this chapter, we will investigate what causes changes in the motion of objects. We will also discuss Newton’s three laws of motion and learn how to apply them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.305,
            0.649,
            0.624,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6.1 The Concept of Force"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
